Q: Snowflake - SELECT...INTO in execute block is returning the wrong value A: <p>It is an example of how variable is interpreted as a value or as an identifier. Using a simpler case:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count string;
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select :fieldgroup  
  into :row_count 
  from (select 1) AS s(claim_num);

  return :row_count;
END;

-- claim_num
</code></pre>
<p>vs.</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count string;
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select identifier(:fieldgroup)
  into :row_count 
  from (select 1) AS s(claim_num);

  return :row_count;
END;

-- 1
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>Returning to the original queries, skipping dynamic part they are as follows:</p>
<pre><code>select count(1)
from raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim
group by 'claim_num'  -- string (which is effectively a single group)
having count(1) &gt; 1;

select count(1)
from raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim
group by claim_num   -- identifier - column name
having count(1) &gt; 1;
</code></pre>
<p><strong><a href="https://dbfiddle.uk/lgtcl_gH" rel="nofollow noreferrer">db&lt;&gt;fiddle demo</a></strong></p>
<p>Final query:</p>
<pre class="lang-sql prettyprint-override"><code>DECLARE
    row_count integer;
    table_name varchar default 'raw.claims.s_clm_carrier_claim';
    fieldgroup varchar default 'claim_num';
BEGIN
  select count(1) 
  into :row_count 
  from identifier(:table_name) 
  group by IDENTIFIER(:fieldgroup )
  having count(1) &gt; 1;
  return :row_count;
END;
</code></pre>
<p>More at: <a href="https://docs.snowflake.com/en/sql-reference/identifier-literal" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Literals and variables as identifiers with IDENTIFIER() syntax</a></p>

Q: How to avoid unnecessary evaluations in LINQ queries using OrderBy followed by multiple ThenBy clauses A: <p>I think you can make use of <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.linq.iorderedenumerable-1.createorderedenumerable?view=net-10.0" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>IOrderedEnumerable&lt;TElement&gt;.CreateOrderedEnumerable&lt;TKey&gt;</code></a></p>
<p>adjusted demo code:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>static void Main() {
    var data = new[]
    {
            new { Name = &quot;Alice&quot;, Age = 30 },
            new { Name = &quot;Charlie&quot;, Age = 25 },
            new { Name = &quot;Bob&quot;, Age = 25 },
        };

    var sorted = data
        .OrderBy(x =&gt; Tracer(&quot;First criterion&quot;, x.Age));
        //.ThenBy(x =&gt; Tracer(&quot;Second criterion&quot;, x.Name));

    sorted = sorted.CreateOrderedEnumerable(x =&gt; x.Name, new MyComparer(), false);

    foreach (var item in sorted) {
        Console.WriteLine($&quot;{item.Name} - {item.Age}&quot;);
    }
}

public class MyComparer : IComparer&lt;string&gt; {
    public int Compare(string x, string y) {
        Tracer(&quot;Second criterion&quot;, x);
        return Comparer&lt;string&gt;.Default.Compare(x, y);
    }
}

static T Tracer&lt;T&gt;(string label, T value) {
    Console.WriteLine($&quot;Computing {label} key: {value}&quot;);
    return value;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Comparison is triggered only for items that are not the same. I think this is what you meant.</p>
<p>Output:</p>
<pre><code>Computing First criterion key: 30
Computing First criterion key: 25
Computing First criterion key: 25
Computing Second criterion key: Charlie
Bob - 25
Charlie - 25
Alice - 30
</code></pre>
<p>If you make Charlie be 26, you won't get invocations of the comparer. But logically there still needs to be some comparison for equality that we don't see - which is unavoidable.</p>
